---
/**
 * @file PostHogScript.astro
 * @description Inline PostHog snippet for the web app (SPEC-140 — fix B).
 *
 * Why a snippet, not a React island:
 * Mixing posthog-js via ESM bundle + array.js auto-loader leaves the SDK in a
 * half-initialised state where `window.posthog` is undefined and events never
 * reach `/e/`. The official PostHog Astro guide
 * (https://posthog.com/docs/libraries/astro) recommends this exact snippet
 * pattern so `window.posthog` is the canonical entry point and the SDK's own
 * loader manages the bootstrap.
 *
 * What this script does on every page render:
 * 1. Reads `PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY` + `PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST` from the Astro env
 *    and passes them via `define:vars` to the inline script.
 * 2. Skips entirely in dev mode (so `pnpm dev` never sends events).
 * 3. Skips when the key is empty (so locally-built preview / staging
 *    environments without the secret stay quiet).
 * 4. Loads the official PostHog stub which fetches `array.js` asynchronously
 *    and upgrades `window.posthog` to the real SDK — but DEFERS that fetch
 *    until after `load` + idle (see "Deferred array.js fetch" below).
 * 5. Reads the `cookie-consent` cookie SYNCHRONOUSLY and boots PostHog in a
 *    hybrid mode: `memory` persistence before consent (anonymous, cookieless),
 *    then `localStorage+cookie` once analytics consent is granted.
 * 6. Subscribes to the `cookie-consent:changed` window event dispatched by
 *    `saveConsent()` in `cookie-consent.ts` so capture can toggle mid-session
 *    without a page reload.
 *
 * Why `disable_surveys: true` (HOS-369):
 * posthog-js ALWAYS fetches `static/surveys.js` — having zero surveys in the
 * project, or toggling surveys off in the PostHog dashboard, does not stop it
 * (the SDK still loads the script so `getSurveys()` / `renderSurvey()` keep
 * working for API-presentation surveys). `disable_surveys: true` is the only
 * switch that prevents the request. Hospeda uses no surveys: zero references
 * anywhere in `apps/web`, and zero surveys configured in any of the three
 * PostHog projects (hospeda-staging, hospeda-web-prod, hospeda-prod). Measured
 * on a staging trace (mobile, 4x CPU, Slow 4G): one request plus ~6 ms of main
 * thread. REMOVE THIS FLAG before shipping any PostHog survey — with it set,
 * surveys silently never render and the survey APIs return no data.
 *
 * Deferred array.js fetch (HOS-369):
 * `array.js` is ~79 KB over the wire from a THIRD-PARTY origin (DNS + TLS on
 * top of the bytes) and the upstream stub fetches it during head parsing, so it
 * lands squarely inside the LCP window. On a Slow-4G mobile trace of the home
 * page it was the single largest resource competing with the hero image, which
 * is bandwidth-bound (~158 KB/s effective across 134 concurrent resources).
 *
 * The fetch is therefore deferred to `load` + `requestIdleCallback`, WITHOUT
 * deferring the stub itself. That distinction is load-bearing: `trackEvent()`
 * goes through `createBrowserAnalytics` in `@repo/analytics`, whose `capture()`
 * SILENTLY NO-OPS when `window.posthog` has no `capture` method (it is
 * try/catch-wrapped and returns early on a falsy client). Deferring the stub
 * would therefore drop every event fired before idle with no error anywhere.
 * Running the stub synchronously keeps `window.posthog` queueing exactly as it
 * does today; only the network request moves.
 *
 * HOW the fetch is held back: the stub is handed a minimal `document` PROXY
 * instead of the real `document`. It creates its <script> through the proxy and
 * inserts it before `getElementsByTagName('script')[0]` — which the proxy
 * answers with an anchor node living in a detached DocumentFragment. A script
 * element inserted into a detached fragment is NOT "already started", so no
 * fetch happens; moving it into `document.head` later starts it normally. This
 * keeps the upstream minified stub byte-for-byte untouched (editing it is how
 * the `define:vars` incident below shipped a silent prod failure) — the only
 * change is the argument it receives.
 *
 * The flush has a FAIL-SAFE: if the proxy did not capture a `script[src]` (a
 * future upstream stub could restructure how it injects), it builds the script
 * itself from `api_host` and appends it. Analytics degrading to "loads a bit
 * early" is acceptable; degrading to "never loads, no error" is not.
 *
 * Known trade-off: `capture_performance.web_vitals` now starts observing after
 * idle. The web-vitals library uses buffered PerformanceObservers so LCP/FCP
 * entries are still picked up retroactively, but attribution fidelity for very
 * late-firing metrics may drop slightly. Accepted for a 79 KB critical-path win.
 *
 * Call sites (search, accommodation detail, signup, contact-host, newsletter)
 * use `trackEvent()` from `@/lib/analytics/posthog-client` which is a thin
 * wrapper around `window.posthog?.capture(...)`.
 */

import { POSTHOG_INLINE_SNIPPET } from './posthog.snippet';

// The env reads, the render gate and the snippet itself live in
// `posthog.snippet.ts` (HOS-807), so the CSP hashes the exact bytes rendered
// here. `null` means "do not render" (dev, or no key).
const snippetBody = POSTHOG_INLINE_SNIPPET;
---

{
    snippetBody !== null && (
        <script is:inline set:html={snippetBody} />
    )
}
